  <title>Interpret Slope and <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>-intercept: Mini-Lesson Review</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00679</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Interpret Slope and y-intercept: Mini-Lesson
      Review</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c3f9ad34-f1af-419a-9f01-062d9358e147</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>

    <note class="mini-lesson-question" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Question #3: Interpret Slope and y-Intercept</title>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
        <para id="para-00001"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_OR_U3_Q3"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>
    </note>

    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Interpret Slope and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-Intercept </title>
      <para id="para-00003">Many real-world applications are modeled by linear equations. Often, when a linear
        equation model uses real-world data, different letters are used for the variables, instead
        of using only <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. The variable names remind us of what quantities are being
        measured.</para>
      <para id="para-00004">The equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>40</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is used to estimate the temperature in degrees
        Fahrenheit, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, based on the number of cricket chirps, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, in one minute. The graph
        shows the relationship.</para>

        <media alt="graph y=¼ x+40, x axis from 0 to 48 label x axis as n, y axis from 0 to 55 label as T">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_08_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
        
      <para id="para-00005">What do the slope and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept represent?</para>
      <para id="para-00006">Answer: The slope is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> and represents that for every 4 chirps per minute, the
        temperature increases by 1 degree. The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>- intercept means when there are zero chirps,
        the temperature is 40 degrees.</para>
  

      <note class="try" id="note-00001">
      <title> Try It: Interpret Slope and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts </title>
      <para id="para-00007">Loreen has a calligraphy business. The equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1.8</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>35</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> models the relation
        between her weekly cost, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, in dollars and the number of wedding invitations, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>,
        that she writes. The graph shows the relationship.</para>
        <media alt="art the graph of y=1.8x+35, x from 0-16 labeled n, the \(y\)-axis from 0 to 60 labeled C">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_08_02.jpg"/>
        </media>

      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00008">What do the slope and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept represent?</para>
          <para id="para-00009">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00010">Here is how to interpret the slope and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept:</para>
          <para id="para-00011">The slope is 1.8 and represents the cost increases by $1.80 for 1 invitation. The
            <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept means the cost for zero invitations is $35.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </note></section>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--inserted
      mini review question Begin --><section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003">
     <title>Check Your Understanding</title>
        <exercise id="_7d1e11f8-758a-414f-a89f-9643bafb909c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00012">Sam drives a delivery van. The equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.5</m:mn><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>60</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> models the relation
              between his weekly cost, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, in dollars and the number of miles, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, that he
              drives. The graph shows the relationship.</para>
            <para id="para-00013">Find and interpret the meaning of the slope and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.</para>
              <media alt="">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_08_03.jpg"/>
              </media>

              <para id="para-00024">Multiple Choice:</para>
              <list list-type="enumerated" number-style="lower-alpha" id="list-00001">
              <item><para id="para-00025">The slope, 0.5, means that the weekly cost, C, increases by $0.50 when the number of miles driven, m, increases by 1. The C-intercept means that when the number of miles driven is 0, the weekly cost is $60.</para></item>
              <item><para id="para-00026">The slope, 0.5, means the weekly cost, C, increases $0.50 a week. The C-intercept is 60, which means that every mile costs $60.</para></item>
              <item><para id="para-00027">The slope, 0.5, means that for every half mile, m, the cost, C, increases by 1. The C-intercept means that when the number of miles driven is 0, the weekly cost is $60.</para></item>
              <item><para id="para-00028">The slope, 0.5, means that the weekly cost, C, decreases by $0.50 when the number of miles driven, m, increases by 1. The C-intercept means that when the number of miles driven is 60, the weekly cost is $0.</para></item>
            </list>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00014">Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The slope is the change in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> over
              the change in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>0.5</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>. Looking at the graph, the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept, 60,
              is when the miles are 0.  The answer is the slope, 0.5, means that the weekly cost,
              <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, increases by $0.50 when the number of miles driven, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, increases by 1. The
              <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>C</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept means that when the number of miles driven is 0, the weekly cost is
              $60.</para>
              <para id="para-00015">Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The slope is the change in 𝐶
                over the change in 𝑚
               . The answer is the slope, 0.5, means that the weekly cost, 𝐶
               , increases by $0.50 when the number of miles driven, 𝑚
               , increases by 1. The 𝐶
               -intercept means that when the number of miles driven is 0, the weekly cost is $60.</para>
              <para id="para-00016">Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The slope is positive so the cost will increase per mile. The 𝐶
                -intercept is the weekly cost when the miles are 0. The answer is the slope, 0.5, means that the weekly cost, 𝐶
                , increases by $0.50 when the number of miles driven, 𝑚
                , increases by 1. The 𝐶
                -intercept means that when the number of miles driven is 0, the weekly cost is $60.</para>
              <para id="para-00017">The slope, 0.5, means that the weekly cost, 𝐶
                , increases by $0.50 when the number of miles driven, 𝑚
                , increases by 1. The 𝐶
                -intercept means that when the number of miles driven is 0, the weekly cost is $60.</para>
              <para id="para-00018">That’s correct! Check yourself: For every mile that Sam drives, the cost increases $0.50. The weekly cost is the 𝐶
                -intercept which is $60, when zero miles are driven.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--inserted
      mini review question END -->
